WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.374
Operators monitor the indicators their approved system exposes.

00:00:03.374 --> 00:00:07.058
These may include receiver warnings, satellite status, accuracy estimates,

00:00:07.058 --> 00:00:10.742
signal-quality summaries, flight-mode changes, home-point messages, and telemetry trends.

00:00:10.742 --> 00:00:13.607
Satellite count alone is not a verdict.

00:00:13.607 --> 00:00:16.472
Geometry, quality, obstruction, and receiver design matter.

00:00:16.472 --> 00:00:20.156
A high count can coexist with a poor solution,

00:00:20.156 --> 00:00:24.659
while a temporary low count may have a benign environmental explanation.

00:00:24.659 --> 00:00:25.887
Compare independent cues.

00:00:25.887 --> 00:00:31.617
Does the map agree with visible landmarks, aircraft movement, compass or heading, known route,

00:00:31.617 --> 00:00:33.664
camera view, and crew observations?

00:00:33.664 --> 00:00:38.166
Disagreement raises concern even when no single cue identifies the cause.

00:00:38.166 --> 00:00:41.031
Trends are more useful than isolated values.

00:00:41.031 --> 00:00:46.352
Record when the anomaly began, where the aircraft was, which mode was active,

00:00:46.352 --> 00:00:52.083
what changed, and whether behavior recovered after landing or moving away from the area.

00:00:52.083 --> 00:00:55.357
Preserve logs, warnings, screenshots, timestamps, weather, site details,

00:00:55.357 --> 00:00:58.632
aircraft configuration, and crew notes according to policy.

00:00:58.632 --> 00:01:04.362
Do not manipulate the system or repeat the flight merely to reproduce suspected interference.

00:01:04.362 --> 00:01:05.181
Classify carefully.

00:01:05.181 --> 00:01:09.683
A suspicious indicator is not a confirmed jamming or spoofing incident.

00:01:09.683 --> 00:01:13.776
Reports should state observed symptoms, confidence, benign alternatives, safety actions,

00:01:13.776 --> 00:01:17.460
and evidence available for qualified technical or regulatory review.
